    <div class="verdict-card"><p class="form-kicker">2 \xB7 DOES ${h.toUpperCase()} COVER SOLO + HELPER?</p><h3>${u}</h3><p>Your <b>${money(e.todaySpend)}/month budget today</b> becomes <b>${money(e.spend65)}/month at 65 in ${g}</b>, after ${65-e.age} years of modelled inflation. Both spending and income below are <b>future-dollar illustrations</b>.</p><div class="gap-timeline">${b(65,g,e.spend65,t.gap65)}${b(75,C,e.spend75,t.gap75)}</div><details class="verdict-calculation"><summary>Why are these amounts different from today\u2019s budget?</summary><p>At 65: (${money(e.todaySpend)} \u2212 ${money(Math.min(300,e.todaySpend))} fixed helper levy) \xD7 1.03<sup>${65-e.age}</sup> + ${money(Math.min(300,e.todaySpend))} = ${money(e.spend65)}/month. After 65, the model also increases the care provision with age.</p><p>CPF income is a scenario too. FRS grows by an assumed 3.5% a year to your age 55; ERS is twice that target. Published 2026 male Standard Plan payouts are scaled using that target-growth factor. The result is held level from 65; it is not a CPF Board quote for ${g}.</p></details><p class="verdict-note">Viewing: <b>${m[t.key]}</b>. ${t.key==="salary"?"This uses projected salary CPF up to FRS. Compare the fully funded FRS or ERS scenarios below.":"This assumes the CPF target is fully funded. The extra savings required are shown below."} Actual balances, future rules and pricing can change the answer.</p></div>
